{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The Collatz conjecture is unsettled; the covering principle is the transcendence of log 3 / log 2, which precludes the existence of non-trivial cycles.","counterconditions":["Discovery of a counter-example cycle via high-performance computing.","Proof that Baker's Theorem bounds are not tight enough to exclude cycles for very large n."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"A non-trivial cycle requires an equality of the form 3^k * n + C = 2^m * n, implying 3^k / 2^m is extremely close to 1. The 'covering principle' here is the linear forms in logarithms (Baker's Theorem), which provides a lower bound on |k log 3 - m log 2|, suggesting that the gap between powers of 2 and 3 grows such that no integer n can satisfy the cycle condition.","optional_refs":["ea3e77fc44e0f0904c83d2e0126207d7481695f7885ffed149b23c4f277e17f9","39a1357d58d82dc17b90a7be44f899717b5a4d608145c2dc53311a408d8df678"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The Collatz conjecture is unsettled and likely undecidable because the problem is isomorphic to a universal Turing machine.","counterconditions":["A proof that the specific coefficients (3 and 1) in 3n+1 constrain the function enough to remove its universality.","A formal proof of the conjecture (e.g., via a new method in analytic number theory)."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Following the work of John Conway on Collatz-like functions, the 3n+1 map can be viewed as a specific instance of a general class of functions where the 'halting' problem (reaching 1) is undecidable. The covering principle is that the map's local behavior (parity) encodes a computation whose termination cannot be determined by a general algorithm.","optional_refs":["36f37fce12fd832574b46b7780c283940e479e57b89feae0db15a8f002f1b760"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The Collatz conjecture is unsettled; the covering principle is an ergodic mixing property of the 2-adic integers.","counterconditions":["Proof that the map is not ergodic on Z_2.","Discovery of a divergent trajectory (n -> infinity) that escapes the 2-adic attraction."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The Collatz map can be extended to the ring of 2-adic integers Z_2. If the map is ergodic on Z_2, then the 'typical' trajectory will visit every neighborhood, and the attraction to 1 is a consequence of the density of integers that fall into the 4-2-1 sink under the 2-adic topology.","optional_refs":["ea3e77fc44e0f0904c83d2e0126207d7481695f7885ffed149b23c4f277e17f9"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The Collatz conjecture is unsettled; the covering principle is the 'probabilistic drift' of the geometric mean of the multiplier.","counterconditions":["Proof that a subset of integers exists where the parity sequence is biased toward odds, overcoming the 0.75 drift.","Proof that 'almost all' is insufficient to prove 'all'."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"On average, an odd step (3n+1) followed by at least one even step (n/2) results in a multiplier of ~1.5 * 0.5 = 0.75. Because the expected value of the log of the sequence decreases linearly, almost all trajectories must trend toward 1.","optional_refs":["39a1357d58d82dc17b90a7be44f899717b5a4d608145c2dc53311a408d8df678"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"If the 'covering principle' is indeed related to the distribution of {k log 3 / log 2}, then the problem is not about integers, but about the diophantine approximation properties of the ratio of the logarithms of the two operators. This would shift the search from search-for-counterexample to search-for-bound.","why_keep_this":"Synthesizes the analytic approach (SCR_007) with the school's demand for a covering principle."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Does the 2-adic extension of the Collatz map allow for a proof of 'no cycles' other than 4-2-1?","related_refs":["SCR_001"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}